00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.650
So today, we're going to move on

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.470
to a fairly different world than the world

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.330
we've been living in.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.590
And this will be a world we'll be living in for quite

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:39.290
a few lectures.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.970
But before I do that, I want to get back to just finish up

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.870
something that Professor Grimmsen started.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.470
You may recall he talked about family trees.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.350
And raised the question, was it actually possible to

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.310
represent all ancestral relationships as a tree?

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.910
Well, as a counter example, I'm sure some of you are

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.170
familiar with Oedipus Rex.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.950
For those of you who are not, I'm happy to give you a

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.710
plot summary at the end of the lecture.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.950
It's a rather bizarre plot.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.670
But it was captured in a wonderful song by Tom Lair.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.530
The short story is Oedipus ended up marrying his

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.310
mother and having four children.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.210
And Tom Lair, if you've never heard of Tom Lair, you're

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.510
missing one of the world's funniest songwriters.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.070
And he had a wonderful song called Oedipus Rex.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.770
And I recommend this YouTube as a way to go and listen

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.150
to it.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:43.690
You can gather from the quote what the story is about.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.430
I also recommend the play, by the way.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.610
It's really kind of appalling what goes on, but it's

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:50.950
beautiful.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.010
All right, back to the main topic.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.890
Here's the relevant reading.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:04.410
A small bit from later in the book and then chapter 14.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.690
You may notice that we're not actually going through the book

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:08.150
in order.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.610
And the reason we're not doing that is because we're trying

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.970
to get you information you need in time to do problem

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:14.310
sets.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:17.210
All right.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:22.630
So the topic of today is really uncertainty.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:28.670
And the fact that the world is really annoyingly hard to

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:37.210
understand, this is a signpost related to 6.0002, but we

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.030
won't go into too much detail about it.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.050
We'd rather things were certain, but in fact, they

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.030
usually are not.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:51.930
And this is a place where 6.0002 diverges from the

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.250
typical introductory computer science course, which

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.250
focuses on things that are functional.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.350
Given an input, you always get the same output.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:02.710
It's predictable.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.870
And we like to do that because that's easier to teach.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.590
But in fact, for reasons we'll be talking about, it's not

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.470
nearly as useful if you're trying to actually write

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.930
computations that help you understand the world, you

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.950
have to face uncertainty head on.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.490
An analogy is for many years people believed in Newtonian

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:29.170
mechanics.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.730
I guess they still do in 801 maybe.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:39.050
That every effect is a cause, and Apple falls from the

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.390
tree because of gravity, and you know where it's going to

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.190
land, and the world can be understood causally.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.110
And people believe this really for quite a long time.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:55.010
Most of history, until the early part of the 20th

00:03:55.010 --> 00:04:00.390
century, when the so-called Copenhagen doctrine was put

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:06.870
forth, the doctrine there from Bohr and Heisenberg, two

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.490
very famous physicists, was one of what they called

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.330
causal nondeterminism.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.270
And their assertion was that the world, at its very

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:22.630
most fundamental level, behaves in a way that you

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:23.410
cannot predict.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.550
It's OK to make a statement that x is highly likely to

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.230
occur, almost certain to occur.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.330
But for no case can you make a statement x will occur.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.010
Nothing has a probability of one.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.810
This was hard for us to imagine today when we all know

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.790
quantum mechanics, but at the turn of the century this was

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.510
a shocking statement.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.870
And two other very well-known physicists, Albert Einstein

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.890
and Schrodinger, basically said, no, this is wrong.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.750
Bohr, Heisenberg, you guys are idiots.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:00.810
It's just not true.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.670
They probably didn't call them idiots.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.550
And this is most exemplified by Einstein's famous quote

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.770
that God does not play dice, which is indicative of the

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.110
fact that this was actually a discussion that permeated not

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.930
just the world of physics, but society in general.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.750
People really turned it into literally a religious issue,

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:22.690
as did Einstein.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.190
Well, so now we should ask the question, does it

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:27.890
really matter?

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.010
And to illustrate that, I need two coins.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.950
I forgot to bring any coins with me.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:35.670
Anyone got a coin they can lend me?

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.130
All right.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.210
Now, this is where I see how much the students trust me.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.110
Do I get a penny?

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.310
Do I get a silver dollar?

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:47.230
All right, so what have we got here?

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:52.450
All right, this is someone who's entrusting me with two

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.430
quarters, not so bad.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.170
So we'll take these two quarters, and we'll shake

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:01.570
them up, and we'll put them down on the table.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.430
And now we'll ask a question.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:11.330
Do we have two heads, two tails, or one head and one tail?

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.130
So who thinks we have two heads?

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.290
Who thinks we have two tails?

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.510
Who thinks we have one of each?

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.950
All right, well, clearly everyone except a few people,

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.590
for example, the Indians fan, who clearly believe in the

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:34.890
counterfactual, made the most probabilistic decision.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.370
But in fact, there's no nondeterminism here.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:41.850
I know the answer, right?

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:48.010
And so in some sense, it doesn't matter whether it's

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.690
deterministic, because in fact, it's not causally

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:51.910
nondeterministic.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:53.230
The answer is quite clear.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.530
But you don't know the answer.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.950
And so whether or not the world is inherently

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.670
unpredictable, the fact that we never have complete

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:10.190
knowledge of the world suggests that we minus well

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:12.450
treat it as inherently unpredictable.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:18.130
And so this is called predictive nondeterminism.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.830
And this really is what's going to underline pretty much

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:23.550
everything else we're going to be doing here.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:31.370
All right, no comments about that?

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.990
All right, I wouldn't do that to you.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:37.470
Thank you.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.050
I know you were wishing to get an interest on the money,

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:43.010
but you don't get any.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:49.990
All right.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:52.370
What was that?

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:55.070
All right.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.050
So when we think about nondeterminism in

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.430
computation, we use the words stochastic process.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:07.490
And that's any process that's ongoing in which the

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:12.490
next state depends upon the previous states in some

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:13.310
random element.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.470
So typically, up till now, when we've written code, what

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:22.050
one line of code did depended only on what the previous

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.150
lines of code did.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:24.350
There was no randomness.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.490
Here we're going to have randomness.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.230
And we can see the difference if we look at these

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.570
two specifications of rolling a die.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.730
The first one returns an int between 1 and 6 is what

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.390
I'll call underdetermined.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:45.050
By that, I mean you can't tell what it's going to return.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.210
Maybe it will return a different number each time you

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.090
call it, but it's not required to.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.210
Maybe it will return 3 every time you call it.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.730
The second specification requires randomness.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:01.790
It says it returns a randomly chosen int, so it

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:04.230
requires a stochastic implementation.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.430
All right, let's look at how we implement a

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.730
random process in Python.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.770
We start by importing the library random.

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.010
This is not to say you can import any random

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:19.010
library you want.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.170
It's to say you import the library called random.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:23.670
Let me get my pen out of here.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.520
And we'll use that a lot.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.120
And then we're going to use the function in random

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.740
called random.choice.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.160
It takes as an argument a sequence, in this case a

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.580
list, and randomly chooses one member of the list.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.760
And it chooses it uniformly.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.570
It's a uniform distribution.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:57.040
And what that means is that it's equally probable that it

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.120
will choose any number in that list each time you call it.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.980
We'll later look at distributions that are

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.780
not uniform, not equally probable, where

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:07.520
things are weighted.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.440
But here it's quite simple.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:10.400
It's just uniform.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:16.260
And then we can test it using test roll.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:21.760
Take some number of n and rolls the die that many times

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.840
and creates a string telling us what we got.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:34.300
So let's consider running this on, say, test roll of five.

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:39.660
And we'll ask the question, if we run it, how probable is

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:44.000
it that it's going to return a string of five ones?

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:50.930
How do we do that?

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.950
Now I know how many people here are either in 6041 or have

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:55.910
taken 6041?

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:57.150
Raise your hand.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:10:59.590
Oh, good.

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:01.910
So very few of you know probability.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:03.250
That helps.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.350
All right, so how do we think about that question?

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:13.390
Well, probability, to me at least, is all about

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:16.910
counting, especially discrete probability, which is what

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:17.770
we're looking at here.

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:23.250
What you do is you start by counting the number of

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:30.360
events that have the property of interest and the number of

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.760
possible events and divide one by the other.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:41.650
So if we think about rolling a die five times, we can

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.810
enumerate all of the possible outcomes of five rolls.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.950
So if we look at that, what are the outcomes?

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:52.750
Well, I could get five ones.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.550
I could get five ones, four ones and a two, or four ones

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.090
and a three, skip a few.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:04.230
The next one would be three ones, a two and a one, and a

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.630
two and a two, and finally, at the end, all sixes.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:13.710
So remember we looked before at when we were looking at

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:17.650
optimization problems about binary numbers, and we said we

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.830
could look at all the possible choices of items in the

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.730
knapsack by vector of zeros and ones.

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:27.170
We said how many possible choices are there?

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:30.350
Well, it depended on how many binary numbers you could get

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:31.910
in that number of digits.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.670
Well, here we're doing the same thing, but instead of

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:37.710
base two, it's base six.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:45.250
And so the number of possible outcomes of five rolls is

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:45.850
quite high.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.350
How many of those are five ones?

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:51.850
Only one of them, right?

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:58.230
So in order to get the probability of five ones, I

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:00.170
divide one by six to the fifth.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:04.770
Does that make sense to everybody?

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:09.850
So in fact, we see it's highly unlikely.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:13.130
Probability of five runs is quite small.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.800
Now, suppose we were to ask about the probability of

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:18.540
something else.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:27.660
Instead of five ones, say five, three, four, two, one, it

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:31.500
kind of looks more likely than that than five ones in a row.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:32.640
But of course it isn't, right?

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.200
Any specific combination is equally probable.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:38.460
And there are a lot of them.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.860
So all the probability we're going to think about, we

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.580
could think about this way as simply a matter of

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.100
counting, the number of possible events, the number of

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.280
events that have the property of interest, in this case

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.540
being all ones, and then simple division.

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:03.140
Given that framework, there are three basic facts about

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:06.180
probability we're going to be using a lot of.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:13.620
So one, probabilities always range from zero to one.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:16.680
How do we know that?

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:18.680
Well, we've got a fraction, right?

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:23.640
And the denominator is all possible events.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:28.340
The numerator is the subset of that that's of interest.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:35.940
So it has to range from zero to the denominator, and that

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:38.700
tells us that the fraction has to range from zero to one.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.280
So one says it's always going to happen zero, never.

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:50.490
So if the probability of event occurring is p, what's

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:52.470
the probability of it not occurring?

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.850
This follows from the first bullet.

00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:01.830
It's simply going to be one minus p.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:08.520
This is a trick that we'll find we use a lot because it's

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:11.140
often the case when you want to compute the probability of

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:11.980
something happening.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.480
It's easier to compute the probability if it's not

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.760
happening and subtract it from one.

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:21.420
And we'll see an example of that later today.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.200
Now here's the biggie.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:32.080
When events are independent of each other, the probability

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:36.340
of all of the events occurring is equal to the

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:40.200
product of the probabilities of each of the events occurring.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:52.360
So if the probability of A is 0.5 and the probability of

00:15:52.360 --> 00:16:00.940
B is 0.4, the probability of A and B is what?

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:07.490
0.5 times 0.4.

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:08.830
You guys can figure that out.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:11.430
I think it's 0.2.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.740
So you'd expect that, right, that it should be much smaller

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.320
than either of the first two probabilities.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.180
This is the most common rule at something we use all the

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:24.860
time in probabilities, the so-called

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:25.900
multiplicative law.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:34.190
We have to be careful about it, however, in that it

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:38.030
only holds if the events are actually independent.

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:45.250
Two events are independent if the outcome of one has no

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:46.950
influence in the outcome of the other.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:53.670
So when we roll the die, we assume that the first roll,

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:56.830
the outcome was independent of the second roll was

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.550
independent of the first roll, independent of the fourth roll.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:03.310
When we looked at the two coins, we assumed that heads

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:05.970
and tails of each coin was independent

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:06.950
of the other coin.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.610
I didn't, for example, look at one coin and make sure

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:11.690
that the other one was different.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:20.260
The danger here is that people often compute probabilities

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:23.400
assuming independence when you don't actually

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:24.620
have independence.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.220
So let's look at an example.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:33.700
For those of you familiar with American football, the

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.080
New England Patriots and the Denver Broncos are two

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.120
prominent teams.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:41.500
And let's look at computing the probability of whether

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.440
one of them will lose on a given Sunday.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:48.560
So the Patriots have a winning percentage of 7 of 8.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:51.560
They've won seven of their eight games so far, and the

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:52.660
Broncos six of eight.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:57.820
The probability of both winning next Sunday, assuming

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:01.240
that this is indicative of how good they are, we can get

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:02.480
with the multiplicative rule.

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:07.060
So it's 7 over 8 times 6 over 8 or 42 over 64.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.660
We could simplify that fraction, I suppose.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:12.880
All right, does that make sense?

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:17.800
So this is probably a pretty good estimate of both of them

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:18.860
winning next Sunday.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:24.740
So the probability of at least one of them losing is 1

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:25.440
minus that.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:31.430
So here's an example of why we often use the 1 minus rule.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.910
Because we could compute the probability of both of them

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:39.590
winning by simply multiplying.

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:42.690
And we subtract that from 1.

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:47.170
However, what about Sunday, December 18th?

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:47.870
What's the probability?

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:54.380
Well, as it happens, that day the Patriots are playing

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:55.020
the Broncos.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.780
So now suddenly the outcomes are not independent.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:06.900
The probability of one of them losing is influenced by the

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:08.160
probability of the other winning.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:09.940
Right?

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:13.700
So you would expect the probability of one of them

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:18.420
losing is much closer to 1 than 22 over 64, which is

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:19.000
about a third.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.740
So in this case it's easy, but as we'll see as we get

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:30.640
through the term, there are lots of cases where you have

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.840
to work pretty hard to understand whether or not two

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.380
events really are independent.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:39.260
And if you get it wrong, you get a totally bogus answer.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.000
A third versus one is a pretty big difference.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:48.420
By the way, as it happens, the probability of the

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:49.940
Broncos losing is about 1.

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:58.270
All right, let's go look at some code.

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:03.710
And we'll go back to our dice, because it's much easier

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:06.450
to simulate dice games than it is to simulate

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.270
football games.

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:15.150
So here it is, and we're going to talk a lot about

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:15.810
simulations.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.090
So here rather than rolling the die, I've

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:20.410
written a program to do it.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.630
We've already seen the code for rolling a die.

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:32.370
And so to run the simulation, typically what we're

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:33.950
doing here is I'm giving you the goal.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.330
For example, are we going to get five ones?

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:45.110
The number of trials, each trial in this case will be, say,

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:46.130
of length five.

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:51.910
So I'm going to roll the same die 5 times, say, 1,000

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:52.730
different times.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:56.170
And then just some text as to what I'm going to print.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:01.910
Almost all the simulations we look at are going to start

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:03.910
with lines that look a lot like that.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:09.050
We're going to initialize some variable, and then we're

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.690
going to run some number of trials.

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:20.760
So in this case, we're going to get from the length of the

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:23.940
goal, so if the goal is five ones, then we're going to

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.220
roll the die five times.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:27.760
If it's 10 runs, we'll roll the die 10 times.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:35.480
So this is essentially one trial, one attempt, right?

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:40.160
And then we'll check the result.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:45.760
And if it has the property we want, in this case, it's

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:49.260
equal to the goal, then we're going to increment the total,

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.480
which we initialized up here, by one.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.500
So we'll keep track, which is just the counting.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:00.280
The number of trials that actually meet the goal.

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.320
And then when we're done, what we're going to do is

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:09.560
divide the number that met the goal by the number of trials.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:14.380
Exactly the counting argument we just looked at, and then

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:15.380
we'll print the result.

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:22.890
Almost every simulation we look at is going to have this

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:23.350
structure.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:27.790
But be an outer loop, which is the number of trials, and

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:30.630
then inside, maybe we'll have a loop, or maybe it won't,

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:31.750
will be a single trial.

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:34.770
We'll sum up the results, and then we'll divide by the

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:35.490
number of trials.

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:37.390
All right, let's run it.

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:42.830
All right.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.820
So a couple of things are going to go on here.

00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:58.860
If you look at the code, as we've looked at it before,

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:02.600
what you're seeing is I'm computing the estimated

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:06.180
probability by the simulation, and I'm comparing it to the

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:09.460
actual probability, which we've already seen how to compute.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:14.130
So if you look at it, there are a couple of things to

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:14.550
look at.

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:20.230
The estimated probability is pretty close to the actual

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:22.830
probability, but not the same.

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:26.270
Let's go back to the PowerPoint.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:32.580
Here are the results.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:38.320
And there are at least two question ways by this result.

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:41.360
First of all, how did I know that this is what would get

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:41.740
printed?

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.200
Remember, this is random.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.460
How did I know that the estimate, well, there's

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:53.200
nothing random of the actual probability, but how did I

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:55.920
know that the estimated probability would be zero?

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:58.220
And why did it print it twice?

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:00.440
Because I messed up the PowerPoint, anyway.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:02.980
So how do I know what would get printed?

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:06.840
Well, a confession.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:14.240
Random.choice is not actually random.

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:17.920
In fact, nothing we can do in a computer is

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:18.960
actually random.

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:24.160
You can prove that it's impossible to build a computer

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:26.820
that actually generates truly random numbers.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.680
What they do instead is generate numbers that are

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:33.840
called pseudorandom.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:42.940
How do they do that?

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:48.560
They have an algorithm that, given one number, generates

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.240
the next number in a sequence.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:56.480
And they start that algorithm with a seed.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:04.160
Now, typically, they get that seed by reading the clock of

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:04.720
the computer.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:09.280
So most computers have a clock that, say, keeps track of the

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.940
number of microseconds since January 1, 1978.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:15.480
I don't know if that's still true.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:16.760
That's what UNIX used to do.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:22.480
So the notion is, you start your program, there's no

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:25.380
way of knowing how many microseconds have elapsed.

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:29.380
And so you're getting a random number to start the process.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:34.100
Since you don't know where it starts, you don't know what

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:35.780
the second number is, you don't know what the third

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.600
number is, you don't know what the fourth number is.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:42.680
And so it's predictably nondeterministic, because

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:44.740
you don't know what the seed is going to be.

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:50.220
Now, you can imagine that this makes programs really

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.020
hard to debug.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.720
Every time you run it, something different could happen.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:58.440
Now, we'll see often you want them to be unpredictable.

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:01.040
But for now, we want them to be predictable.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:03.040
Makes it easier to prepare a PowerPoint.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:13.510
So what you have, if we go is a command, you can call

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:19.850
random.seed and give it a value and say, I don't want

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:21.630
you to just choose some random seed.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.430
I want you to use zero as the seed.

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:27.370
For the same seed, you always get the same sequence

00:26:27.370 --> 00:26:28.570
of random values.

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:32.850
And so what I've done is I've set the seed to be, I think,

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:33.890
zero in this case.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:36.590
Not because there's anything magic about zero.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:37.710
It's just sort of habit.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.770
But it made it predictable.

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:44.030
As you write programs with randomness in, and when

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:46.610
you're debugging it, you will almost surely want to start

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:49.410
by setting random.seed to a value.

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:50.630
So you get the same answer.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.730
But make sure you debug it with more than one value

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.870
of this, so you didn't just get lucky with your seed.

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:57.590
All right.

00:26:58.070 --> 00:27:00.570
So that's how I knew what would get printed.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:06.330
The next question is, why did the simulation

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:07.430
give me the wrong answer?

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:13.630
The actual probability is 3 zeros and 1, 2, 8, 6.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.430
But it estimated the probability of zero.

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:19.910
Why is it wrong?

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:25.840
Well, let's think about this.

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:28.480
I ran 1,000 trials.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.220
What does it mean to say the probability is zero?

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:36.060
It means it tried it 1,000 times and didn't ever

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:37.880
get a sequence of 5 ones.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:42.020
So the numerator of the division at the bottom was zero.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:45.620
Hence, the answer is zero.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:46.880
Is this surprising?

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:48.280
Well, no.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:54.520
Because if that's the actual probability of getting 5 ones,

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:58.420
it's not very shocking that in 1,000 trials it never

00:27:58.420 --> 00:27:58.860
happened.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:03.590
Now, it's not a surprising result.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:08.350
And so we have to be careful when we run these things

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:11.690
to understand the difference between what's, in this case,

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:15.730
an actual probability and what statisticians call

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.170
a sample probability.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:28.080
So what we got with the sample was zero.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:30.720
So what's the obvious thing to do?

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:34.840
If you're doing a simulation of an event

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:38.960
and the event is pretty rare, you

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:42.320
want to try it on a very large number of trials.

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:44.920
So let's go back to our code.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:55.680
And we'll change it to, instead of 1,000, a million.

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:00.670
You can see up here, by the way, where I set their seed.

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:02.750
Now let's run it.

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:18.480
We did a lot better.

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:21.560
If we look at here, our estimated probability,

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:26.980
it's 3,0128, still not quite the actual probability,

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:28.580
but darn close.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.620
And maybe if I'd done 10 million, it

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:32.580
would have been even closer.

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:38.710
So if you're writing a simulation to compute

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:42.950
the probability of an event and the event is moderately rare,

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:46.550
then you better run a lot of trials

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.910
before you believe your estimated probability.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:55.370
In a week or so, we'll actually look at that more

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:57.350
mathematically and say, what is a lot?

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:58.850
How do we know what is enough?

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:10.950
All right.

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:12.990
What are the morals here?

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.510
Moral one, I've just told you, takes a lot of trials

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.630
to get a good estimate of the frequency of a rare event.

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:25.630
Moral two, we should always, if we're getting an

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:29.850
estimated probability, know that and probably say that

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:32.150
and not confuse it with the actual probability.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:37.850
The third moral here is it was kind of stupid to do a simulation

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:41.730
since it was a very simple closed form answer

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:44.910
that we could compute that would really tell us

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:47.250
what the actual probability is.

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:49.170
Why even bother with the simulation?

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:54.970
Well, we're going to see why now because simulations can

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:55.810
be very useful.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.310
Let's look at another problem.

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:01.910
This is the famous birthday problem.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:02.970
Some of you have seen it.

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:06.090
What's the probability of at least two people in a group

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:07.190
having the same birthday?

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:12.010
There's a URL at the bottom that's pointing to a Google form.

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:15.790
I'd like, please, all of you have a computing device

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:18.070
to go to it and fill out your birthday.

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:22.490
It's anonymous, so we won't know how old you are.

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:22.950
Don't worry.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:24.010
Actually, it's only the date.

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:25.150
It's not the year.

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:26.730
All right.

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:33.930
So suppose there were 367 people in the group, roughly the

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:38.310
number of people who took the 60001600 midterm.

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:43.550
If there are 367 people, what's the probability of at

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:44.990
least two of them sharing a birthday?

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:52.550
One, by something called the pigeonhole principle.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:56.330
You've got some number of holes, and if you have

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:59.210
more pigeons than holes, two pigeons have to share a hole.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:02.670
What about smaller numbers?

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:08.050
Well, if we make a simplifying assumption that each birth

00:32:08.050 --> 00:32:12.210
date is equally likely, then there's actually a nice

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:13.970
closed form solution for it.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:22.380
Again, this is a question where it's easier to compute

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:24.840
the opposite of what you're trying to do when subtract

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:25.600
it from 1.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:31.700
And so this fraction is giving the probability of two

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:33.420
people not sharing a birthday.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.720
The proof that this is right is a little bit elaborate, but

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:41.100
you can trust me, it's accurate.

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:44.920
But it's a formula, and it's not that complicated a

00:32:44.920 --> 00:32:49.620
formula, though numbers like 366 factorial are big.

00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:51.800
All right.

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:57.300
So let's approximate a solution.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.800
We'll write a simulation and see if we get the same answer

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:02.240
that that formula gave us.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:05.040
So here's the code for that.

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:11.730
Two arguments, the number of people in the group and the

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:15.750
number that we asking do they have the same birthday.

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:20.630
So since I'm assuming for now that every birthday is

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:23.420
equally likely, the possible dates range from

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:28.520
1 to 366 because some years have a February 29th.

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:35.730
I'll keep track of the number of people born in each date by

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:37.070
starting with none.

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:41.930
And then for p in the range of number of people, I'll make a

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:46.110
random choice of the possible dates and increment that

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:47.650
element of the list by 1.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.100
And then at the end we can say look at the maximum

00:33:52.100 --> 00:33:55.660
number of birthdays and see if it's greater than or

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:57.220
equal to the number of same.

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:01.210
So that tells us that.

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:07.250
And then we can actually look at the birthday problem, the

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:09.970
number of people, the number of same, and as usual the number

00:34:09.970 --> 00:34:10.510
of trials.

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:16.890
So number of hits is 0 for t in range number of trials.

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:21.990
If same date is true, then we'll increment the number of

00:34:21.990 --> 00:34:26.390
hints by 1 and then as usual divide by the number of trials.

00:34:27.910 --> 00:34:34.490
And we'll try it for people in 10, 20, 40, and 100 people.

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:41.710
And then just we'll print the estimated probability and the

00:34:41.710 --> 00:34:47.470
actual probability computed using that formula I showed

00:34:47.470 --> 00:34:47.730
you.

00:34:48.210 --> 00:34:51.390
I've not shown you, but I've imported a library called

00:34:51.390 --> 00:34:55.170
math because it is a factorial implementation that's

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:58.270
way faster than the recursive one that we've seen before.

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:24.790
All right, let's run it and we'll see what we get.

00:35:24.970 --> 00:35:28.450
So for 10 the estimated probability is 0.11.

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:34.790
So as you can see the estimates are really pretty good.

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:39.530
Once again we have this business that for 100 we're

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:42.450
estimating 1 when the real answer is 0.

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:44.170
Many, many, many nines.

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:48.670
But again, this is sample probability, just means in the

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:54.430
number of trials we did, every one for 100 people there was a

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:54.970
shared birthday.

00:35:55.770 --> 00:36:00.230
This is a number that usually surprises people as to why with

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:02.330
100 people the probability is so high.

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:06.510
But we could work out the formula and see it.

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:09.310
And as you can see the estimates are pretty

00:36:09.310 --> 00:36:10.750
good from my simulation.

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:22.240
All right, now we're going to see why we did a simulation in

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:22.860
the first place.

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:27.460
Suppose we want the probability of three people

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:29.120
sharing a birthday instead of two.

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:36.750
It's pretty easy to see how we change the simulation.

00:36:37.090 --> 00:36:38.230
I even made it a parameter.

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:41.310
I just changed the number two to a number three.

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:45.010
The math, on the other hand, is ugly.

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:50.670
Why is the math so much uglier for three than for two?

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:55.450
Because for two, the complementary problem, the

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:59.470
number we're subtracting from one, is simply the question of

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:01.190
are all birthdays different?

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:06.110
So did two people share a birthday?

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:09.550
Is one minus or does everybody have a different birthday?

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:15.030
On the other hand, for three people, the complementary

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.870
problem is a complicated disjunct, a bunch of ores.

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:23.810
Either all birthdays are distinct, or two people share a

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:27.990
birthday and the rest are distinct, or there are two

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:30.170
groups of two people sharing a birthday and

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:31.170
everything is distinct.

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:34.750
So you can see here there's a lot of possibilities.

00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:39.810
And so it's one minus now a very complicated formula.

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:42.990
And in fact, if you try and look how to do this, most

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:46.310
people will tell you don't bother, here's kind of a

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:47.170
good approximation.

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:50.130
But the math gets very hairy.

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:56.010
In contrast, changing the simulation is dead easy.

00:37:56.870 --> 00:37:57.710
We can do that.

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:13.690
So if we come over here for the code, all I have to do is

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:15.070
change this 2 to a 3.

00:38:24.790 --> 00:38:27.610
And I'm going to leave in this code, which is the

00:38:27.610 --> 00:38:32.330
wrong code, computing the actual probability now for two

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:33.910
people sharing rather than 3.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:36.490
Because I want to make it easy for you to see the

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:39.510
difference between what happens when we look at 3

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.110
shared rather than 2 shared.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:54.370
And I get invalid syntax.

00:38:55.730 --> 00:38:56.550
That's not good.

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:00.610
That's what happens when I type in real time.

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:09.490
Why do I have invalid syntax?

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:12.010
Pardon?

00:39:13.910 --> 00:39:14.990
One person at Anna.

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:21.360
Oh, that's not a good line.

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:40.300
So now we see that if we get, say, to n equals 100, for 2

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:41.660
you'll remember it was 0.99.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:44.220
But for 3 it's only 0.63.

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:49.960
So we see going from 2 sharing to 3 sharing gets us a

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:51.260
radically different answer.

00:39:52.160 --> 00:39:52.920
Not surprisingly.

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:57.280
But we also, and the real thing I wanted you to see is

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:59.620
how easy it was to answer this question to the

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:00.260
simulation.

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:06.240
And that's a primary reason we use simulations to get

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:10.360
probabilistic questions rather than sitting down in the

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:12.400
pencil and paper and doing fancy probability

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:13.220
calculations.

00:40:14.060 --> 00:40:16.980
Because it's often way easier to do a simulation.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:22.120
We can see that in spades if we look at the next question.

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:28.830
Let's think about this assumption that all birthdays

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:29.870
are equally likely.

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:34.770
Well, as you can see, this is a chart of how common

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:37.170
birth dates are in the US, a heat map.

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:45.460
And you'll see, for example, that February 29th is quite an

00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:46.400
uncommon birthday.

00:40:47.620 --> 00:40:49.700
So we should probably treat that differently.

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:54.620
Somewhat surprisingly, you'll see that July 4th is a

00:40:54.620 --> 00:40:56.140
very uncommon birthday as well.

00:40:57.180 --> 00:40:59.700
It's easy to understand why February 29th.

00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:02.720
The only thing I can figure out for July 4th is

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:05.080
obstetricians don't like working on holidays.

00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:09.260
And so they induce labor sometime around the second or

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:11.980
so to the third, so they don't have to come to work on the

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:12.700
fourth to the fifth.

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:16.820
Sounds a horrible thought, but I can't think of any other

00:41:16.820 --> 00:41:18.320
explanation for this anomaly.

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:22.780
Probably if you look at it, see Christmas Day is not so

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:23.340
common either.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:28.020
So now the question, which we can answer, since you've

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:31.380
all filled out this form, is how exceptional are MIT

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:31.920
students?

00:41:32.560 --> 00:41:35.060
We like to think that you're different in every respect.

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:39.040
So are your birthdays distributed differently than

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:39.680
other dates?

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:41.740
Have we got that data?

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.760
All right, so now we'll go look at that.

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:50.670
We should have a heat map for you guys.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:54.240
This one.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:04.710
Yeah.

00:42:05.590 --> 00:42:07.550
All right, so here it is.

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:14.540
And we can see that they don't seem to be banded quite

00:42:14.540 --> 00:42:18.620
as much in the summer months, probably says more about

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:20.260
your parents than it does about you.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:27.520
But you can see that indeed we do have, wow, we have a

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:29.980
day where there are five birthdays that look like,

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:30.380
or no.

00:42:32.870 --> 00:42:33.350
Wow.

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:38.130
You want to raise your hand if you're born on

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:39.450
February 12th?

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:48.670
All right, so you are exceptional that you lie

00:42:48.670 --> 00:42:49.830
about when you're born.

00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:57.100
But if you hadn't lied, I think we would have still

00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:58.620
seen the probabilities would hold.

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:00.700
How many people were there, do we know?

00:43:08.540 --> 00:43:11.160
146 people, 112 unique birthdays.

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:15.980
So indeed, the probability does work.

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:28.220
OK, so we know you're exceptional in a funny way.

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:32.960
Well, you can imagine how hard it would be to adjust

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:37.180
the analytic model to account for a weird

00:43:37.180 --> 00:43:38.600
distribution of birth dates.

00:43:40.180 --> 00:43:42.920
But again, adjusting the simulation model is easy.

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:47.740
I could have gone back to that heat map I showed you of

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:51.900
birthdays in the US and gotten a separate probability for

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:53.800
each day, but I was too lazy.

00:43:54.980 --> 00:44:00.700
And instead what I observed was that we had this a few

00:44:00.700 --> 00:44:03.500
days, like February 29th, highly unlikely.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:10.100
And this band in the middle of people who were conceived in

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:11.520
the late fall and early winter.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:18.580
So what I did is I duplicated some dates.

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:24.980
So the 58th day of the year, February 29th, occurs only

00:44:24.980 --> 00:44:25.440
once.

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:31.380
The dates before that, I said let's pretend they occur

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:32.080
four times.

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:35.220
What only matters here is not how often they occur, but the

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:36.160
relative frequency.

00:44:40.570 --> 00:44:46.570
And then the dates after that occur four times, except for

00:44:46.570 --> 00:44:50.170
the dates in that band, which is going to have occur yet

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:50.870
more often.

00:44:52.130 --> 00:44:55.990
So now, and don't worry about the exact details here, but

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:58.930
what I'm doing is simply adjusting the simulation to

00:44:58.930 --> 00:45:03.770
change the probability of each date getting chosen by same

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:04.090
date.

00:45:07.110 --> 00:45:08.970
And then I can run the simulation model.

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:14.030
And again, with a very small change to code, I've modeled

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:17.230
something that's mathematically enormously complex.

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:20.090
I have no idea to actually do this

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:22.370
probability mathematically.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:26.110
But the code is, as you can see, quite straightforward.

00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:35.320
So let's go to that here.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:45.630
So what I'm going to do is comment this one out and

00:45:45.630 --> 00:46:02.890
uncomment this more complicated set of dates and see

00:46:02.890 --> 00:46:03.370
what we get.

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:16.100
And again, it changes quite dramatically.

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:18.740
You might remember before it was around, I think, 0.6

00:46:18.740 --> 00:46:21.760
something for 100, and now it's 0.75.

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:25.760
So getting away from the notion that birthdays are

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:28.820
uniformly distributed to saying some birthdays are more

00:46:28.820 --> 00:46:31.300
common than others, again, dramatically

00:46:31.300 --> 00:46:32.380
changes the answer.

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:36.240
And we can easily look at that.

00:46:43.040 --> 00:46:47.740
So that gets us to the big topic of simulation models.

00:46:49.220 --> 00:46:53.180
It's a program that describes a computation that provides

00:46:53.180 --> 00:46:56.900
information about the possible behaviors of a system.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:47:00.340
I say possible behaviors because I'm particularly

00:47:00.340 --> 00:47:02.900
interested in stochastic systems.

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:10.430
They're descriptive, not prescriptive, in the sense that

00:47:10.430 --> 00:47:12.830
they describe the possible outcomes.

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:16.850
They don't tell you how to achieve a possible outcomes.

00:47:18.250 --> 00:47:21.870
This is different from what we looked at earlier in the

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:24.050
course, where we looked at optimization models.

00:47:25.630 --> 00:47:29.490
So an optimization model is prescriptive.

00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:34.390
It tells you how to achieve an effect, how to get the

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:38.790
most value out of your knapsack, how to find the

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:40.810
shortest path from A to B in a graph.

00:47:41.850 --> 00:47:45.570
In contrast, a simulation model says, if I do this,

00:47:46.190 --> 00:47:47.150
here's what happens.

00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:50.450
It doesn't tell you how to make something happen.

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:54.510
So it's very different, and it's why we need both.

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:57.550
Why we need optimization models and

00:47:57.550 --> 00:47:58.910
we need simulation models.

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:03.790
We have to remember that a simulation model is only in

00:48:03.790 --> 00:48:05.330
approximation to reality.

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:09.510
I put in an approximation to the distribution of birth

00:48:09.510 --> 00:48:11.370
dates, but it wasn't quite right.

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:15.410
And as the very famous statistician George

00:48:15.410 --> 00:48:19.570
Bach said, all models are wrong, but some

00:48:19.570 --> 00:48:20.730
are actually very useful.

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:25.850
In the next lecture, we'll look at a useful class of

00:48:25.850 --> 00:48:26.210
models.

00:48:27.450 --> 00:48:29.270
When do we use simulations?

00:48:30.250 --> 00:48:33.010
Typically, as we've just shown, to model systems

00:48:33.010 --> 00:48:36.510
that are mathematically intractable, like the birthday

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:41.750
problem we just looked at, in other situations to extract

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:44.430
intermediate results, something that happens along

00:48:44.430 --> 00:48:45.530
the way to the answer.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:50.890
And as I hope you've seen, that simulations are used

00:48:50.890 --> 00:48:54.570
because we can play what if games by

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:56.130
successively refining it.

00:48:56.970 --> 00:49:00.330
We started with a simple simulation that assumed that

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.850
we only asked the question of, did two people share a

00:49:02.850 --> 00:49:03.150
birthday?

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:07.670
We showed how we could change it to ask to three people

00:49:07.670 --> 00:49:08.410
share a birthday.

00:49:09.530 --> 00:49:13.410
We then saw that we could change it to assume a

00:49:13.410 --> 00:49:17.330
different distribution of birth dates in the group.

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:20.670
And so we can start with something simple and we

00:49:20.670 --> 00:49:25.070
get it ever more complex to answer questions what if.

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:32.160
We're going to start in the next lecture by

00:49:32.160 --> 00:49:35.640
producing a simulation of a random walk.

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:39.500
And with that, I'll stop and see you guys soon.

